data: {"type":"data-evidence","data":{"id":21,"productId":"openmrs","generation":"g-b59a21","repository":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-module-webservices.rest","path":"omod/src/test/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/controller/openmrs1_10/OrderController1_10Test.java","revision":"HEAD","startLine":346,"code":["\t\tList<Order> originalActiveOrders = orderService.getActiveOrders(patient, null, null, null);","\t\tassertTrue(originalActiveOrders.contains(orderToRevise));","\t\t","\t\tEncounterService es = Context.getEncounterService();","\t\tDate date = new Date();","\t\tEncounter encounter = new Encounter();","\t\tencounter.setEncounterType(es.getEncounterType(1));","\t\tencounter.setPatient(patient);","\t\tencounter.setEncounterDatetime(date);","\t\tes.saveEncounter(encounter);","\t\t","\t\tSimpleObject revisedOrder = new SimpleObject();","\t\trevisedOrder.add(\"type\", \"testorder\");"],"highlightedLines":[349,350,351,352,353,354,355]}}

data: {"type":"data-evidence","data":{"id":22,"productId":"openmrs","generation":"g-b59a21","repository":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-module-webservices.rest","path":"omod/src/test/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/controller/openmrs1_9/EncounterController1_9Test.java","revision":"HEAD","startLine":527,"code":["\t        throws Exception {","\t\tPatient patient = Context.getPatientService().getPatientByUuid(RestTestConstants1_9.PATIENT_WITH_ENCOUNTER_UUID);","\t\t","\t\tEncounter enc = Context.getEncounterService().getEncounterByUuid(RestTestConstants1_9.ENCOUNTER_UUID);","\t\tContext.getEncounterService().voidEncounter(enc, \"some reason\");","\t\tContext.getEncounterService().saveEncounter(enc);","\t\t","\t\t//list of non voided encounters","\t\tList<Encounter> encs = Context.getEncounterService().getEncountersByPatient(patient);","\t\tAssertions.assertEquals(2, encs.size());","\t\t","\t\tMockHttpServletRequest req = request(RequestMethod.GET, getURI());","\t\treq.addParameter(\"patient\", RestTestConstants1_9.PATIENT_WITH_ENCOUNTER_UUID);","\t\treq.addParameter(\"includeAll\", \"true\");","\t\t","\t\tSimpleObject result = deserialize(handle(req));","\t\tList<Object> encounters = result.get(\"results\");","\t\tAssertions.assertEquals(2, encounters.size());","\t}","\t","\t@Test","\tpublic void shouldFetchOnlyNonVoidedPatientEncountersIfIncludeAllParamIsSetToFalse() throws Exception {","\t\tPatient patient = Context.getPatientService().getPatientByUuid(RestTestConstants1_9.PATIENT_WITH_ENCOUNTER_UUID);","\t\t","\t\tEncounter enc = Context.getEncounterService().getEncounterByUuid(RestTestConstants1_9.ENCOUNTER_UUID);","\t\tContext.getEncounterService().voidEncounter(enc, \"bcbcb\");","\t\tContext.getEncounterService().saveEncounter(enc);","\t\t","\t\t//list of non voided encounters","\t\tList<Encounter> encs = Context.getEncounterService().getEncountersByPatient(patient);"],"highlightedLines":[530,531,532,533,534,535,536,537,538,539,540,541,542,543,544,545,546,547,548,549,550,551,552,553]}}

data: {"type":"data-answer","data":{"kind":"grounded","generation":"g-b59a21","paragraphs":[{"text":"In `openmrs-core`, `EncounterService.saveEncounter` is the one method that writes an encounter (a clinical visit record: patient, date, type, obs, orders, diagnoses, providers) to the database [1]. Its real implementation is in `EncounterServiceImpl.saveEncounter` [2]: it checks edit rights, assigns a visit to new encounters, copies the encounter's date/location/patient onto its obs and orders, delegates the actual write to `dao.saveEncounter` [3], then cascades to order groups, orders, obs, conditions, allergies and diagnoses.","citations":[1,2,3]},{"text":"If that behaviour changed, the affected code in `openmrs-module-webservices.rest` (the module that exposes OpenMRS core data over a REST API) is the three production call sites below. Nothing else in the module calls it directly.","citations":[]},{"text":"## Confirmed affected locations","citations":[]},{"text":"**1. `EncounterResource1_8.save` — the `/encounter` create and update endpoint**\nThe v1_8 encounter resource calls it directly [4]. This `save` is invoked by the generic CRUD machinery, `DelegatingCrudResource.create` for `POST /encounter` [5] and `DelegatingCrudResource.update` for `POST /encounter/{uuid}` [6]. Any change to date/location propagation, obs handling, order saving or diagnosis saving in the core method changes what this endpoint persists. Note the resource deliberately wires up obs and orders via property setters before saving, so it depends on the core method's cascades [7].","citations":[4,5,6,7]},{"text":"**2. `EncounterResource1_9.save` — overrides the above for OpenMRS 1.9–2.1**\nThis subclass re-implements `save`, so its version calls the service and ignores the parent's return value [8]. It first force-sets the parent encounter on each `EncounterProvider` (a \"hack\" noted in the comment) because without it providers were stored without an `encounter_id` [9]. This is a behaviour-sensitive dependency: a core change to how encounters and their providers are linked on save could break this workaround.","citations":[8,9]},{"text":"**3. `EncounterResource2_2` inherits both**\nIt extends `EncounterResource1_9` [10], so it uses call site 2 unchanged and only adds the `diagnoses` property. Since the core method also saves diagnoses [11], this class is doubly affected: both its own added property work and the core diagnosis cascade matter.","citations":[10,11]},{"text":"**4. `EncounterProviderResource1_9` — the nested `/encounter/{uuid}/encounterprovider` endpoint**\nThis is a sub-resource of `EncounterResource1_9` [12] and calls the service in two places:","citations":[12]},{"text":"| Method | Purpose | Calls `saveEncounter` at |\n|---|---|---|\n| `delete` | void an encounter provider, then re-save the parent encounter | [13] |\n| `save` | add a provider to the encounter, then re-save it | [14] |","citations":[13,14]},{"text":"Both paths save the *whole encounter*, not just the provider, so they inherit every cascade in the core method — including obs re-validation for existing obs — for a request that the client thinks only touches providers. The `save` path also relies on the encounter's provider collection being correctly persisted, then re-scans it to return the persisted `EncounterProvider` [15].","citations":[15]},{"text":"## Likely but indirect","citations":[]},{"text":"`EncounterResource1_8.purge` voids/purges rather than saving, so it is not a caller [16]. `EncounterResource1_8.delete` / `undelete` use `voidEncounter` / `unvoidEncounter` [17], and the search path uses read-only methods [18]. `DiagnosisResource2_2.save` goes through `DiagnosisService.save`, not `EncounterService` [19], so it is only indirectly affected if diagnoses submitted inside an encounter payload change behaviour. I did not find any other production caller; `EncounterService` is also used in this module for encounter types, roles and searches, which do not touch `saveEncounter` [20].","citations":[16,17,18,19,20]},{"text":"## Tests that would exercise the changed behaviour","citations":[]},{"text":"Production callers above are joined by test callers: `OrderController1_10Test` [21] and `EncounterController1_9Test` [22]. These would fail loudly if the save semantics changed, which is useful as a canary.","citations":[21,22]},{"text":"## Flow","citations":[]},{"text":"```mermaid\n---\ntitle: Encounter save path from REST to core\n---\nflowchart TD\n  post[\"POST or update on /encounter\"] --> crud[\"DelegatingCrudResource create or update\"]\n  crud --> r18[\"EncounterResource1_8.save\"]\n  crud --> r19[\"EncounterResource1_9.save inherits to 2_2\"]\n  sub[\"/encounter/uuid/encounterprovider\"] --> prov[\"EncounterProviderResource1_9 save or delete\"]\n  r18 --> svc[\"EncounterService.saveEncounter\"]\n  r19 --> svc\n  prov --> svc\n  svc --> dao[\"dao.saveEncounter\"]\n  svc --> cascades[\"order groups orders obs conditions allergies diagnoses\"]\n```","citations":[]},{"text":"## What I could not check","citations":[]},{"text":"The reference search was run against the default branch of both repositories and `EncounterService.saveEncounter` is called through the static `Context.getEncounterService()` lookup rather than injected references, so a caller could in principle resolve the service at runtime in a way static search misses. The other selected O3 front-end and core-module repositories are outside this question's scope and were not searched; if they consume the `/encounter` endpoint they would be affected only through the REST contract, not through this method directly.","citations":[]}],"gaps":[],"partialReasons":[],"abstention":null,"suggestions":[]}}

